// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
//
// Expected: c1c1u1 — a blind signature scheme whose blinding factor is derived
// from the request identifier the client also publishes. The factor is not
// secret, so the blinding is not blinding: the signer recovers the message it
// was not supposed to see, and any observer links the blinded request to the
// signature that came back.
//
// blind_factor_leaked.vp makes the same point with an explicit `leaks`. This
// one is the version that occurs in practice, where nobody leaked anything and
// the factor was simply derived from something already public — which is
// harder to notice and exactly as fatal.
//
// The unlinkability verdict is the one worth reading. The shared-origin
// witness cannot reach `final_sig` at all: it descends only through terms the
// attacker can recompute, and not being recomputable without `sk` is the whole
// point of a signature. The link is recognition rather than reconstruction —
// the attacker builds `factor`, opens `blinded` for `message`, and
// SIGNVERIF(gsk, message, final_sig) succeeds where a signature over any other
// request would fail. unlink_signature_recognized.vp isolates that rule
// against a sealed counterweight.

attacker[passive]

principal Alice[
	knows public request_id
	knows private message
	factor = HASH(request_id)
	blinded = BLIND(factor, message)
]

Alice -> Signer: blinded

principal Signer[
	knows private sk
	gsk = PUBKEY(sk)
	blind_sig = SIGN(sk, blinded)
]

Signer -> Alice: [gsk], blind_sig

principal Alice[
	final_sig = UNBLIND(factor, message, blind_sig)
	leaks final_sig
]

queries[
	confidentiality? message
	confidentiality? factor
	unlinkability? blinded, final_sig
]
